This README covers two implementations of a Queue data structure in C++: the first using the C++ Standard Template Library (std::queue) and the second using a custom singly linked list.

A queue follows the First-In, First-Out (FIFO) principle, where elements are inserted at the rear and removed from the front.

OVERVIEW OF IMPLEMENTATIONS

1. STL Queue Implementation
This implementation wraps the built-in std::queue container. It defines helper functions to enqueue (push), dequeue (pop), and check if the queue is empty.
2. Linked List Queue Implementation
This implementation builds a queue from scratch using dynamic memory allocation and pointers. Node structure holds an integer data value and a pointer to the next node. Pointers front and rear track the head and tail of the queue. Additional functionality includes a display function to traverse and print all elements.

TIME AND SPACE COMPLEXITY

STL Queue Operations:

* Enqueue (push): O(1) time complexity.
* Dequeue (pop): O(1) time complexity.
* Is Empty (Isempty): O(1) time complexity.
* Space Complexity: O(N) where N is the number of elements in the queue.

Linked List Queue Operations:

* Enqueue (push): O(1) time complexity, as insertion occurs directly at the rear pointer.
* Dequeue (pop): O(1) time complexity, as deletion occurs directly at the front pointer.
* Is Empty (Isempty): O(1) time complexity.
* Display (display): O(N) time complexity, as it traverses all nodes from front to rear.
* Space Complexity: O(N) where N is the number of elements, plus pointer overhead per node.

SAMPLE OUTPUT

Program 1 (STL Queue) Output:
Removed: 10
Queue is not empty

Program 2 (Linked List Queue) Output:
10 20 30
Removed: 10
20 30
Queue is not empty
